@node The Idea
@chapter The Idea

Sheet-music PDFs are pictures. Editing pictures of music is a dead end, and
comparing two of them tells you nothing about @emph{what} changed. chantey
is built on one architectural decision:

@quotation
@strong{The PDF is a rendering, not the source.} One canonical MusicXML
file per piece is the single source of truth; audio and notation are both
derived from it.
@end quotation

Everything follows from that. An edit is a change to the canonical file,
so every artifact regenerates consistently. A diff is meaningful, because
two versions of the same piece differ in identifiable measures. And the
comparison page can highlight exactly those measures, because the tool ---
not a human --- computed them.

chantey supplies the pipeline: ingest, inspection, rendering, diffing, and
the comparison page. The musical @emph{judgment} --- what to change ---
stays with people and the scripts they request (@pxref{Editing Scores}).

@node Setup
@chapter Setup

chantey wraps existing engines. Each is checked when needed, with an
actionable error if missing:

@table @strong
@item MuseScore + FluidSynth (apt)
@example
sudo apt install musescore3 fluidsynth fluid-soundfont-gm
@end example
PDF engraving and the piano voice.

@item Audiveris (flatpak) --- only for PDF ingest
@example
flatpak remote-add --user --if-not-exists flathub \
    https://dl.flathub.org/repo/flathub.flatpakrepo
flatpak install -y --user flathub org.audiveris.audiveris
@end example
Optical music recognition. Skip it if your scores exist as MusicXML.

@item hornpipe --- the deterministic synth voice
@example
uv tool install git+https://github.com/mr-kelley/hornpipe
@end example
@end table

chantey itself carries heavy Python dependencies (music21, Verovio) in its
own environment; install it the same way (@code{uv tool install
git+https://github.com/mr-kelley/chantey}) or run it from a clone with
@code{uv run chantey}.

@node The Workflow
@chapter The Workflow

@example
chantey ingest tune.mxl              # -> tune.musicxml (canonical)
chantey info tune.musicxml           # the structural map
#   ... apply the requested edit as a music21 script,
#       writing revised.musicxml ...
chantey diff tune.musicxml revised.musicxml
chantey render tune.musicxml
chantey render revised.musicxml
chantey compare tune.musicxml revised.musicxml
@end example

Open @file{compare.html} in a browser: both versions engraved side by
side, @strong{every measure numbered} (long pieces need cheap references),
the changed measures highlighted in crimson in both columns, and players
for all four audio renders.

Two habits pay off. Run @command{info} first, always --- it is both the
``did ingest work'' check and the reference map when phrasing an edit
request. And run @command{diff} after every edit, @emph{before} rendering
--- it confirms the edit touched exactly what was intended.

@node Commands
@chapter Commands

@section info

@example
chantey info @var{score}
@end example

Title and composer, parts with measure counts, key and time signatures
with their change points, tempo marks, note count, repeat barlines and
voltas.

@section ingest

@example
chantey ingest @var{file} [-o @var{out.musicxml}] [--force]
@end example

Produces the canonical MusicXML. @file{.mxl}/@file{.musicxml} input is
normalized as-is --- @emph{always prefer this path when it exists}:
lossless, nothing to proof. @file{.pdf} input goes through Audiveris OMR
(headless; chantey handles the flatpak sandbox plumbing) and prints the
proofing reminder (@pxref{OMR and Proofing}). Metadata is carried through;
a missing title is backfilled from the filename.

@section render

@example
chantey render @var{score} [--voice piano|synth|both] [--no-pdf]
               [-o @var{dir}] [--force]
@end example

Writes, beside the score or in @var{dir}: @file{@var{stem}.piano.wav},
@file{@var{stem}.synth.toml} + @file{@var{stem}.synth.wav}, and
@file{@var{stem}.pdf}. @xref{The Two Voices}, for what the voices are and
the guarantees they share.

@section diff

@example
chantey diff @var{original} @var{revised} [-v]
@end example

Measure-by-measure comparison of note content (pitches, positions,
durations), per part. Prints the changed-measure list; @option{-v} adds
per-part detail; identical scores are reported as such (exit 0 either
way).

@section compare

@example
chantey compare @var{original} @var{revised} [-o @var{out.html}] [--force]
@end example

One self-contained local HTML file. Changed measures come from
@command{diff} automatically --- never a hand-maintained list. Audio
players appear for whichever rendered WAVs already sit beside the two
scores, so run @command{render} on both first. Identical scores produce a
notice and no page.

All outputs are non-clobbering; @option{--force} overwrites.

@node Editing Scores
@chapter Editing Scores

Editing is deliberately @emph{not} a chantey command. Real requests arrive
in informal musical language --- ``the figure in the bass: B-flat, B-flat,
C, D in eighths; split all but the first into twinned sixteenths,
everywhere it occurs'' --- and every one reduces to the same primitive:
@emph{match a shape, transform it, report the measures}. That is a short
music21 script, not a frozen command surface. The worked example (the
request above, verbatim from real use):

@example
from music21 import converter, beam
import copy

s = converter.parse("tune.musicxml")
for meas in s.parts[1].getElementsByClass("Measure"):
    notes = [el for el in meas.notesAndRests if el.isNote]
    for i in range(len(notes) - 3):
        run = notes[i : i + 4]
        if any(float(n.duration.quarterLength) != 0.5 for n in run):
            continue
        ps = [n.pitch.midi for n in run]
        if ps[1]-ps[0] == 12 and ps[2]-ps[1] in (1,2) and ps[3]-ps[2] in (1,2):
            for el in run[1:]:              # the first note keeps its eighth
                el.duration.quarterLength = 0.25
                el.beams = beam.Beams()
                meas.insert(float(el.offset) + 0.25, copy.deepcopy(el))
s.write("musicxml", fp="revised.musicxml")
@end example

Note the shape match (an octave leap then two rising steps) rather than
literal pitches --- informal descriptions often name the notes but miss
the octave, and figures recur transposed. Report the changed measures back
(via @command{chantey diff}) before rendering, so a too-eager match gets
caught by a human first.

@node OMR and Proofing
@chapter OMR and Proofing

Optical music recognition is the one lossy step in the pipeline, so it
gets honest treatment. Audiveris performs well on clean, born-digital
engravings, acceptably on good scans, poorly on photos and handwriting.

After any PDF ingest:

@enumerate
@item
Run @command{chantey info} --- structural absurdities (missing parts, zero
notes) show up immediately.
@item
Render the transcription back (@command{chantey render}) and compare
against the original PDF @emph{by eye} (the engraving) and @emph{by ear}
(the piano render).
@item
Fix what matters in the canonical MusicXML. It is the source now; the PDF
is history.
@end enumerate

Known loss classes: tempo and expression text, fingerings (occasionally
misread as dynamics), articulation glyphs. Pitches and rhythms survive
well on clean input.

@node The Two Voices
@chapter The Two Voices

@table @strong
@item piano
music21 writes MIDI; FluidSynth renders it with a General MIDI SoundFont.
Realistic instrument sound --- what you listen to for pleasure and for
judging a chord voicing's color.

@item synth
chantey converts the score to a hornpipe score and hornpipe renders it.
Synth-class timbre, but @emph{deterministic}: the same score produces a
bit-identical WAV, every time, which makes renders reproducible and
regressions detectable.
@end table

Two guarantees, both earned from a real defect found during development:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{The voices always agree about form.} Repeats and voltas are
expanded once, identically, for both. (The defect: one voice honored a
chorus repeat, the other flattened it --- 180 vs. 142 seconds of the same
piece.)
@item
@strong{Tempo changes just work.} hornpipe is single-tempo by design, so
the converter resolves every metronome mark itself and emits timing in
absolute seconds. A piece that moves from quarter=100 to quarter=102
mid-score renders correctly in both voices.
@end itemize

The engraved PDF, by contrast, keeps repeat @emph{notation} on the page
--- players read repeats; audio files play them out.

@node Licensed Material
@chapter Licensed Material

Much sheet music is copyrighted --- including modern arrangements of
public-domain tunes (check the fine print; a 1907 song can carry a 2012
arrangement copyright). The rules chantey is built around:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Work privately and locally on lawfully obtained copies. chantey never
publishes, uploads, or hosts anything; the comparison page is a local
file. Sharing decisions belong to you alone.
@item
Keep working scores and everything derived from them in a directory your
version control ignores, so licensed material never enters repository
history.
@end itemize

@node Reference
@chapter Reference

@section Environment

@table @code
@item CHANTEY_SOUNDFONT
Path to the General MIDI SoundFont for the piano voice. Default:
@file{/usr/share/sounds/sf2/FluidR3_GM.sf2}.
@end table

@section Exit status

0 --- success (including ``identical scores'' outcomes). 1 --- error:
unparseable score, missing binary, failed external tool, refused
overwrite. 2 --- usage error.

@section Output naming

For a score @file{@var{stem}.musicxml}: @file{@var{stem}.piano.wav},
@file{@var{stem}.synth.toml}, @file{@var{stem}.synth.wav},
@file{@var{stem}.pdf}, and (from @command{compare}) @file{compare.html}
beside the revised score.

@bye
